\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 These results suggest a 
\f1\b mixed, high-agency profile
\f0\b0 : you may be comfortable with strategy, pressure, competition, and difficult decisions, while also possessing meaningful humility, empathy, and ethical intent. The central leadership challenge is likely not a lack of values, but ensuring that your behaviour under pressure remains aligned with those values.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Important limitation:
\f0\b0  This is a developmental interpretation, not a clinical diagnosis or personality classification. Scores labelled \'93psychopathy,\'94 \'93Machiavellianism,\'94 or similar do not establish a disorder. Without the questionnaire\'92s norms, validation data, subscales, and scoring thresholds, a score such as 68/100 should be read only as a relatively elevated signal within this profile, not as a percentile or clinical cutoff. Self-report scores can also be affected by social desirability, response style, current mood, context, and one\'92s reference group. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.02309/full"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \strokec3 [frontiersin.org]}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://odad.org/storage/files/article/2b89439b-af7c-45eb-81b8-bd5511179518-sg9gUMcpXTplwiDs/sXenVOE4WuR2xUR.pdf"}}{\fldrslt \cf3 \strokec3 [odad.org]}}\
I am assuming that 
\f1\b 50 is the instrument\'92s conceptual midpoint and that higher scores mean more of the named tendency
\f0\b0 .\
